Resolve the CSS-wide inherit keyword on every property and shorthand (CSS Cascade 4): a declaration whose whole value
is inherit takes the parent's computed value. It parsed as invalid and was dropped, so a lower declaration such as the
user-agent `select { color }` stood where a page's `select { color: inherit }` reads its container's.
An inherited property's inherit is its parent's computed value already. One that does not inherit takes the declaration
giving the parent its value (an inherit there defers further up; the initial value where the parent declares none),
computed in the declaring element's font size, zoom and viewport; vertical-align and the border widths, whose computed
values read other properties of their element, copy the parent's computed value. The zoom factor, registered as the
inherited effective zoom, takes the parent's own. Specified-value readers (GetProperty, the local-property reads of
layout and ComputedValues, transitions) see that declaration, and a child declaring inherit on a property that does not
inherit is dirtied by its parent's change of it.
A shorthand's inherit sets every longhand, with the ones its value syntax here leaves out: line-height and font-kerning
for font, the border-image longhands for border; removing the shorthand removes them too.

